    c11_body_m: {
      chapter: "c11",
      location: "Clinic · night desk",
      speaker: "Mia",
      pages: [
        "\"Your shoulders are carrying a different year,\" she says. \"Not gym. Not archive. Someone has been teaching you to take up space like a threat and then apologize for it with clothes. The aftershave isn't yours. You used to smell like toner and the river. Now you smell like a building that washes its air.\"\n\nShe taps the bruise. \"And this. And the way you don't blink when the generator hiccups. Men I grew up with flinched at that sound because it meant the lights might die. You filed it. That's not masculinity. That's employment.\" She says employment the way other people say cancer: as a word that rearranges a kitchen. You want to tell her the aftershave was assigned. Assignment is not a comfort. Assignment is how you got the bruise. She waits for a joke you do not have. The fluorescent tube ticks. The moth, elsewhere, has not learned. Neither have you.",
        "If Vale has begun any work, she will see it: a softness at the jaw that was not there, a skin that looks too even under clinic light, the faint medical sweetness that is not citrus lotion. She does not say CHRYSALIS again. She does not need to. Her gaze is the word.\n\n\"I can treat a bruise,\" she says. \"I cannot treat a person who is being turned into a product while I wrap wrists in a loft that used to make coats. Tell me if I am looking at my friend or at a draft.\" The bay's monitor beeps a bored reminder that other bodies are still negotiating with the night. Mia's gaze does not leave {{him}}. Friendship, at thirty-one, is a diagnostic instrument. You have never been so precisely loved, or so precisely in danger of it.",
      ],
      next: "c11_coffee",
    },

    c11_body_f: {
      chapter: "c11",
      location: "Clinic · night desk",
      speaker: "Mia",
      pages: [
        "\"The walk is new,\" she says. \"Not the shoes. The walk. Someone taught you how to enter a room as if the room had already agreed. Your voice sits differently in your throat. I heard it when you said my name with your eyes. And the coat is cut for a silhouette you didn't used to argue with.\"\n\nShe is not cruel about it. Cruelty would be easier. She is precise. \"If this is yours â if you asked for it â I will learn the new map. If this is a firm wearing you, I need to know how much of you is still answering the door.\" She says map and you think of Vale's slates, of Crowe's hand, of a river stair that had no legend. You keep still because still is the only courtesy you have left that is not a lie about work.",
        "Clinic light is honest in the way hospitals are honest: it makes every edit visible. A mouth that has been trained. A neck that holds itself for cameras. If CHRYSALIS has started, Mia will see the early weather of it: skin, scent, the way {{his}} hands rest as if they belong to a legend instead of a person who used to steal fries.\n\n\"I can be glad you look like you can survive a room of rich people,\" she says. \"I can also hate the reason. I am holding both. That is what adult friendship is. It is heavier than the river.\" A drop from the kettle's old scale ticks on the unseen counter. Time, in clinics, is always dripping somewhere. She hears it and does not move. The walk she named sits in {{his}} hips like a passenger who has paid.",
      ],
      next: "c11_coffee",
    },

    c11_coffee: {
      chapter: "c11",
      location: "Clinic · break alcove",
      speaker: "",
      pages: [
        "She pulls you into the break alcove because the desk is a stage and the alcove is a closet with a kettle. The kettle has scale. The mugs do not match. She makes coffee that tastes like a dare. You drink it because refusing would be another tell.\n\n\"I get off in forty if nobody tries to die,\" she says. \"You can wait in here and be a civilian, or you can tell me the true sentence now so I don't spend the next forty minutes wrapping wrists while imagining your funeral.\" She leans against the counter. The silver chain shifts. A small charm: a cheap coin from the river year. She kept it. You feel that like heat. The mug she gives you has a chip that fits {{his}} thumb. You have drunk from this chip before, in a year when the worst secret was a missed rent. The coffee is worse than the archive's and therefore more honest.",
        "Outside, a tram announces a delay in a voice that has been trained to sound sorry. Inside, the generator does its wrong sound again. Mia hears it and does not pretend. She writes a note on a paper towel and sticks it to the kettle: TELL MAINTENANCE OR I WILL. That is who she is. She leaves evidence of care in rooms that eat evidence.\n\nYou have three roads and they are all ugly. You can protect her by lying well enough that she stays a nurse on Plate Four. You can confess and put her in the blast radius with her eyes open. You can use her â a slot, an alibi, a kind face in a photograph â and tell yourself that using a friend is what the work is. The coffee goes cold while you choose. She watches {{him}} choose and does not help. Helping would be managing, and she has already forbidden that. The alcove's single bulb hums at a frequency that would make a Helix engineer write a ticket. Nobody here writes tickets for light.",
      ],
      next: "c11_ask",
    },

