        "The man at the pharmacy is the man from the alley, or he is the same type: coat too clean, phone at the wrong height, patience that is paid for. When he lifts the phone this time, the lens is obvious. He is not pretending to text. He is taking the two of you in the doorway the way people take monuments.\n\nA second figure sits in a car that has no plate dirt. The window is down a finger's width. You catch a smear of blonde, a mouth that has learned to smile for reports. Maren Pell does not always come to the cheap plates herself. When she does, it means Crowe wants a photograph that can be shown in a nicer room. A civic notice on the pharmacy glass promises flu shots and a smile. The smile has been peeled at the corner. Under it, older paint: a name, a year, a heart. The phone does not care about the heart. The phone wants the two of you in a doorway.",
        "Mia's hand finds {{his}} sleeve. Not clinging. Marking. \"I can go back inside,\" she says. \"I can walk straight at him and ask if he needs a tetanus shot. I can kiss you and make the picture useless as evidence of anything except that we are adults who used to share food. Tell me which kind of stupid you want.\"\n\nThe civic camera on the pharmacy blinks. The man with the phone does not care about civic cameras. He cares about the story the gala will need: {{name}} still has a soft place. Soft places can be rented, threatened, or invited to glass buildings as plus-ones. You have seconds to decide whether Mia is protected, hidden, or spent in the frame. The potatoes are gone, eaten or dropped; you cannot remember which, which is how nights begin to fail. Her fingers on the sleeve are a pulse you did not authorize and will not refuse. The car's idle is too quiet for this plate. Quiet costs money.",
