        "You turn Mia by the shoulders and put her through the door she just locked. She resists for a half-second, pride, then goes, because she can do math. You hear the bolt. You stay on the street alone with the potatoes' grease still on {{his}} fingers and the camera's red blink and the man who came for two people and will have to settle for one.\n\nHe takes the picture anyway. You let him. A single operative in a doorway is a different story than a nurse in the blast. You even give him a bored face, the liaison face, the face that says this plate is a shortcut not a home. Alone, the doorway is a proscenium. You feel ridiculous and correctly used. The man takes two more frames, insurance. You give him the bored face until your jaw aches. Boredom, Kane said, photographs as innocence if you can hold it.",
        "Maren's window rises. You imagine the caption she will write: asset still sentimental, sentimental can be scheduled. You can live with that caption if Mia is a blur behind frosted glass.\n\nShe opens the door a crack. \"I hate you a little,\" she whispers. \"Come around the service alley. I am not staying in my own clinic like a hostage while you pose.\" You go around. The alley smells of the generator's wrongness and frying oil. She is there with her coat and her fury and a look that says protection is not the same as consent, even when it works. The service alley is narrower than memory. Generator heat paints {{his}} cheek. She is whispering swear words that used to be funny when you were twenty-two and are not funny now. You take them anyway. They are the first unmonitored language of the night.",
