        "You put {{his}} arm around Mia's shoulders as if the years had always been this easy. She stiffens, understands, and then â because she is proud, because she is angry, because she will not look like a victim in a Helix album â she leans in and smiles a real-looking smile. The phone takes it. The car takes it. Somewhere in a glass building a file gains a warmth it can spend.\n\n\"You're using my face,\" she says through the smile. \"I will remember the exact temperature of this.\" You tell her that memory is the only leverage civilians get. She says that is a spy sentence and it makes her sick, and she does not step away. A neighbor passes and nods at Mia, not at you, which is correct. You are the visitor. The phone loves the nod; it makes the picture look like a life. Mia's smile does not reach the eye that faces you.",
        "Maren, if it is Maren, lets the window stay down long enough for you to see that she is pleased. Pleased is worse than hostile. Hostile means you are still a problem. Pleased means you are a tool that found its own attachment.\n\nWhen the car leaves, Mia's smile drops like a dressing. \"My slot,\" she says. \"Not because you asked. Because I am not standing on this corner waiting for a sequel. If you needed a photograph, you have it. If you needed me, you had better still need me when the picture is no longer useful.\" You walk off the corner before the car can change its mind. Her shoulder under {{his}} arm is bone and a twelve-hour shift. She counts steps under her breath, a nurse habit, until the numbers become a street she owns. At the next corner she takes {{his}} arm off her shoulders as if removing a dressing. The absence is a temperature. You do not ask to put it back.",
