        "You walk the residential spine like people who have always walked it. Laundry vents breathe detergent and onion. A couple argues about a bill in a language the Stack pretends it doesn't understand. A cat with a torn ear follows you for half a block and then remembers it has a better offer.\n\nMia talks about a patient with a sprained wrist who wanted to go back to a night shift at a packing plant. She talks about the generator. She talks about anything that is not a lens. You let her. The body wants to check reflections. You give it three and then stop, because four is a tell and she is already collecting your tells like symptoms. Someone is frying onions as if the plate still believed in dinner. The smell is almost enough to make you civilian. Mia says the patient's name — first only — and then stops, realizing she has given you a person you could spend. She does not give you another.",
        "Her building is the kind that still has a human at a desk until midnight and then a camera that pretends. The human is asleep with a sports wrap on. Mia nods as if that were security enough. The lift smells of garlic and someone's perfume that is trying to be expensive. On the seventh floor the corridor lights flicker in a pattern you automatically map. She notices you mapping it.\n\n\"If you teach me to see like that, I will not be able to unsee my patients,\" she says. \"So don't. Come in. Take your shoes off. The floor is honest and it will tell on you if you track the street in.\" The sleeping desk-human snores in a key the generator would recognize. Mia's floor button is worn pale. Seven. You have never been here and you have always been here. Both facts sit in the garlic lift and do not fight.",
