        "Mia's slot is six meters by five and has a window that faces the ventilation throat, as promised. She has made it a home anyway: a plant that is surviving on stubbornness, a stack of novels with cracked spines, a chair with a blanket that has been washed into softness. On the wall, a photograph of the river stairs. Two people, twenty-two, squinting. You look like someone who had not yet learned the word legend.\n\nShe puts water on. She does not ask if you want tea. She makes it. The mugs here match. That feels like a thesis. The plant has one new leaf that is paler than the others, an optimism she did not mention. You do not touch it. Touching would be a claim. The river photograph watches {{him}} fail to be twenty-two. Water begins to sound in the kettle, a small domestic weather. She does not look at you while it builds. Building is allowed. Looking, right now, would be another inventory.",
        "\"I have a shift tomorrow and you have a gala,\" she says. \"I saw the thumbnail on your phone when you were pretending not to look at it. Glass. Money. The kind of lighting that makes bruises into accessories.\" She sits on the floor with her back to the bed because the bed would make the room a different sentence. \"You can sleep in the chair. You can tell me to lock you out. You can ask for something you shouldn't. I am too tired to pretend I don't know the menu.\"\n\nThe tea tastes like the citrus lotion, or you are inventing that. Outside, the ventilation throat howls in a way that is almost like weather. You have one more choice that will stick. Distance. More truth. Or the kind of use that happens with mouths and hands when a photograph has already happened and the night wants a body to make the story true.",
