        "You take the chair. She looks briefly disappointed and then briefly relieved, which is the correct pair of feelings. She throws you the washed blanket. It smells like her soap. You do not make a joke. Jokes would pull you back onto the floor.\n\n\"Good,\" she says, and does not sound like she believes the word. \"If I wake up and you're gone, leave the mug in the sink so I know you were a person and not a dream I had because the generator was loud.\" She turns off the main light and leaves the kitchen strip on, a nurse's night-light, a way of keeping the room from becoming a grave. The chair knows other night-shifts: a coat, a book, a person who sat up with a fever that was not theirs. You fit the groove. She turns her face to the wall so that watching her would be a choice, not an accident.",
        "You sit in the dark and listen to her breathe toward sleep. She does not sleep quickly. Neither do you. The ventilation throat writes long sentences. Your phone lights once: Crowe, a time, a dress code, a note that plus-ones are discouraged unless they photograph well. You turn it face down.\n\nAt some point Mia says, into the pillow, \"I still have the voice note. I'm not deleting it. If you get rewritten, I want one file that is still you being a coward about my voice.\" You do not answer. Answering would be a door. You keep the door shut and call it love because tonight you need a word that is not tradecraft. Water moves in a pipe like a small river that learned to live in walls. You count its passes the way you used to count tram stops. Counting is how you do not stand up and ruin the protection you just paid for with a chair.",
