        "You sit on the floor opposite her, the way you sat on the river stairs, and you give her the rest. Helene Crowe. The gala in glass. Senator Ives, fifty-eight, export codes, a daughter he mentions when drunk. The possibility that you will be asked to take him to a room and come out with something that is not consent in any civilian sense. Vale's clinic. The word conversion used like a benefit.\n\nMia's tea goes cold. She does not cry. She looks older than thirty-one and then exactly thirty-one, which is worse. She does not take notes. She is the note. When you say Ives' age she flinches as if age were a wound she could irrigate. When you say conversion she looks at {{his}} throat and then away, granting you the dignity of not being inventoried again. The tea between you goes still, a skin forming, a little civilian death. She does not blow on it. Some things she will let cool rather than pretend they are still drinkable.",
        "\"I want you to come back here after,\" she says. \"Not to report. To be washed. I know how to wash a person who has been in a bad room. That is my whole job.\" She reaches out and this time she holds {{his}} wrist over the bruise, covering it, a human tourniquet. \"If you do the senator, I don't need the details. I need you to remember there is a slot with matching mugs. If you cannot remember, I will come to the glass building and I will be a problem. I am practicing being a problem. The pencil in my hair is sharper than it looks.\"\n\nYou almost laugh. She almost lets you. Then she leans her head on {{his}} shoulder and you sit like that until the ventilation howl becomes a kind of clock. No sex. Something more expensive: a witness.",
