        "She understands the ask before you finish it. Her mouth goes thin, then she sets the mug down as if it were an instrument. \"You want a night that can be mistaken for a life,\" she says. \"You want my bed in the photograph if they come up the stairs. You want my mouth so you can remember you are still someone who can be wanted without a contract.\" She stands. She does not undress for the window. She pulls the curtain, cheap fabric, a civilian defense.\n\n\"I am not your honeytrap,\" she says. \"I am not Ives. I am not Crowe. If we do this, it is because I have wanted to know the true sentence since the river and I am done dying of politeness. You will look at me. You will use my name. If you close your eyes and see a ballroom, I will stop.\"",
        "What follows is not a scene for a file, which is the only reason it almost works. She kisses like a person who has been on her feet for twelve hours: hungry, a little angry, precise about teeth. {{His}} hands find the nick on her thumb, the silver chain, the heat at the back of her neck where the pencil-knot comes down. She pushes {{him}} onto the bed that would have made the room a different sentence and then makes the sentence anyway.\n\nClothes become a pile that still looks like work. Her mouth on {{his}} throat is a claim. When {{he}} is inside the heat of her — or when her hand, her mouth, the wet seam of her takes {{him}} apart according to the body {{he}} has tonight — she does not perform. She watches {{his}} face as if watching a monitor. \"Stay,\" she says, meaning here, meaning this name. {{He}} comes with a sound that is too honest for Helix. She follows with her own hand, forehead hard against {{his}}, and for a minute the slot is only breath and citrus and the ventilation throat going on without you. Then she says, very quietly, \"If they use this, I will still have done it for me. Remember that, so you don't get to be tragic.\"",
